    {
      "id": 182847,
      "name": "Dandolo",
      "username": "Dandolo",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dandolo/{size}/1845_2.png",
      "created_at": "2016-09-24T14:49:00.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p><span>\n[quote=\"Powerhound_2000\"]\n</span></p><p><span>Provocator Tarnis with its wording only cares about the first time any other villain target than itself takes damage which is why it only triggers once. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Wrong Time and Place is not worded in that it cares about the first time any hero target would take damage. &nbsp;</span></p><p><span>\n[/quote]\n</span></p><p><span>I feel like I'm not reading the same words you are.... are you saying the phase \"other than this card\" somehow modifies the meaning of the word \"a\" two words earlier. Why?</span></p><p><span>Both {targets that are villain targets and are not Provocator Tarnis} and {targets that are hero targets} are both subsets of the set of {all targets}. Both act as the subject of the sentence which is otherwise identical save for the object of the sentence {this card}/{Setback}. &nbsp;There is 1 other difference in that Wrong Time and Place has an additional clause to its sentence but that's separated by a conjunction (\"or\") so it should have no impact on the meaning of the first clause.</span></p>",
      "post_number": 7,
