      "bookmarked": false,
      "raw": "<p style=\"line-height: 13.6pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Trebuchet MS&quot;, sans-serif;\">\n[quote=\"Powerhound_2000\"]\n<o:p></o:p></span></p><p style=\"line-height: 13.6pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Trebuchet MS&quot;, sans-serif;\">Provocator Tarnis with its wording only cares about the first time any other villain target than itself takes damage which is why it only triggers once. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Wrong Time and Place is not worded in that it cares about the first time any hero target would take damage. &nbsp;<o:p></o:p></span></p><p style=\"line-height: 13.6pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Trebuchet MS&quot;, sans-serif;\">\n[/quote]\n<o:p></o:p></span></p><p style=\"line-height: 13.6pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Trebuchet MS&quot;, sans-serif;\">I feel like I&#39;m not reading the same words you are.... are you saying the phase &quot;other than this card&quot; somehow modifies the meaning of the word &quot;a&quot; two words earlier. Why?<o:p></o:p></span></p><p style=\"line-height: 13.6pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Trebuchet MS&quot;, sans-serif;\">Both {targets that are villain targets and are not Provocator Tarnis} and {targets that are hero targets} are both subsets of the set of {all targets}. Both act as the subject of the sentence which is otherwise identical save for the object of the sentence {this card}/{Setback}. &nbsp;There is 1 other difference in that Wrong Time and Place has an additional clause to its sentence but that&#39;s separated by a conjunction (&quot;or&quot;) so it should have no impact on the meaning of the first clause.<o:p></o:p></span></p>",
